From b6a05b1218a5a66a52344a7de1eb8caf986f258b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work_5 <adbtrr_work_5@local>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 11:58:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] apps/examples/testcase: Add semaphore scenario test tier,
 phase 5

Phase 5: families D, G and the testable part of family H, plus the robust
mutex scenario deferred from phase 4.

Family D, protocol and flavour, new stc_sem_protocol.c:
- SCN-PIP-01 is the behavioural test section 1.2 of the catalogue says is
  missing. tc_semaphore_sem_setprotocol() asks the kernel to disable
  inheritance and asserts the call returned zero; this asserts that the
  holder's priority does not move.
- SCN-PIP-02 flips the protocol back and requires the boost to return.
  Without it, SCN-PIP-01 would also pass on a kernel where nothing ever
  boosts, so the pair is what distinguishes the two.
- SCN-PIP-03 is the regression for the PRIOINHERIT_FLAGS_DISABLE check
  inside sem_findhighestwaiter(). A holder owns a disabled semaphore and
  a normal one; releasing the normal one makes the restore walk visit the
  disabled entry, whose waiter must not set the holder's priority.
- SCN-PIP-04 asserts that a zero initialised semaphore records no holder,
  so the awakened waiter never becomes a permanent one. That is the
  failure mode sem_setprotocol()'s own documentation describes, and the
  FLAGS_SIGSEM auto-detection is meant to make the manual fix unnecessary.
- SCN-PIP-05 covers PTHREAD_PRIO_INHERIT against PTHREAD_PRIO_NONE and
  checks the mutex count invariant at every step.

Family G, destruction and reset, new stc_sem_destroy.c:
- SCN-DES-01 destroys a semaphore with two live holders that each also
  hold a second one, then makes both post the survivor. That post walks
  each task's holdsem list during the priority restore, so a holder freed
  from the semaphore but left linked on the task faults exactly there.
- SCN-DES-02 destroys a semaphore with a blocked waiter. POSIX leaves
  this undefined and the scenario asserts no particular count, only that
  the undefined case is survivable and the blocked task still deletes
  cleanly, which is what error path cleanup actually needs to know.
- SCN-DES-03 and SCN-DES-04 run inside the driver. sem_reset() is
  declared in tinyara/semaphore.h and is not a syscall, so it cannot be
  reached from user space; the sequence is a new TESTIOC_SEM_RESET_TEST
  handler in os/drivers/os_api_test/kernel/test_sem.c, in the same style
  as the existing tick wait test, triggered from the scenario.
- SCN-DES-05 churns both flavours through init and destroy, checking the
  holder record is freed each cycle.

Family H is partly not implementable, and that is stated rather than
worked around. SCN-BIN-01 to SCN-BIN-03 need a loadable user binary that
can be faulted on demand, which is fixture work rather than test case
work; the manual procedure is written into the header of
stc_sem_destroy.c. SCN-BIN-04, the scope limit that a signalling
semaphore is never registered for binary recovery, is covered in process,
and the run log prints a notice naming what is still missing so the gap
is visible outside the catalogue.

SCN-REC-04, the robust mutex EOWNERDEAD path, is added to
stc_sem_recovery.c under !CONFIG_PTHREAD_MUTEX_UNSAFE. On the reference
platform it reports a skip naming the configuration needed, because
CONFIG_PTHREAD_MUTEX_UNSAFE=y leaves the whole robust mutex
implementation out of the build. Oracle 3 is the interesting one: if the
mutex were released both by pthread_mutex_inconsistent() and by
sem_release_all(), the count would end one too high, which on a
FLAGS_SEM_MUTEX semaphore is an assertion rather than a quiet corruption.

Not build verified: no target toolchain in this environment. The
application sources were syntax checked with gcc -Wall -Wextra against
stub headers in four configurations, including one with
CONFIG_PTHREAD_MUTEX_UNSAFE set to cover the skip path, all clean. The
kernel side driver changes are not covered by that check.
